/* =====================================================================
   TEACHING, SECTION 13 — Psychiatry of Intellectual Disability
   11 items, 13.1.1 to 13.3.7
   ===================================================================== */

var TEACH = (typeof TEACH === "undefined") ? {} : TEACH;

TEACH["13.1.1"] = { free:true,
  simple:"For most of the twentieth century the answer to intellectual disability was a building far from town. The idea that replaced it was simple and radical: give people the same patterns of ordinary life that everyone else has. Not special care. Ordinary life.",
  teach:[
    "Normalisation was formulated by Bengt Nirje in Sweden in 1969: making available to people with intellectual disability the patterns and conditions of everyday life which are as close as possible to the norms of mainstream society.",
    "Nirje's eight patterns include a normal rhythm of the day, a normal rhythm of the week, a normal rhythm of the year, the normal experiences of the life cycle, normal respect for choices, normal sexual patterns, normal economic standards, and normal environmental standards.",
    "Wolf Wolfensberger took the idea to North America and later reframed it as Social Role Valorisation in 1983: the goal is to enable people to occupy socially valued roles, because valued roles bring the good things of life with them.",
    "Wolfensberger's argument was that devalued people are treated badly not because of their impairment but because of the role they are cast in, and that changing the role changes the treatment.",
    "The social model of disability distinguishes impairment, the functional limitation, from disability, the disadvantage created by how society is organised. On this account a wheelchair user is disabled by stairs, not by legs.",
    "The five service accomplishments, from O'Brien: community presence, choice, competence, respect, and community participation. These became the practical audit tool for services.",
    "The scandals drove the policy. Ely Hospital, Cardiff, 1969, exposed by a whistleblower and investigated by Howe, was the first major UK inquiry and led directly to the Hospital Advisory Service.",
    "Better Services for the Mentally Handicapped, 1971, was the first white paper to set targets for reducing hospital places and expanding community provision.",
    "An Ordinary Life, King's Fund, 1980, set out the model of ordinary housing in ordinary streets with support brought to the person, rather than the person brought to the institution.",
    "Valuing People, 2001, was the first white paper in thirty years, built on four principles: rights, independence, choice and inclusion. Valuing People Now followed in 2009.",
    "Person-centred planning replaced service-led assessment: the plan starts from what the person wants their life to be, and the service is arranged around it rather than the reverse.",
    "Direct payments and personal budgets give the person or their family control of the money, and were intended to make choice real rather than notional.",
    "Winterbourne View, exposed by Panorama in 2011, showed systematic abuse in a private assessment and treatment unit and produced the Transforming Care programme, aimed at closing such units and moving people into community support.",
    "Transforming Care has repeatedly missed its targets. The number of people with learning disability and autism in inpatient units has fallen far more slowly than planned, and this is a live policy failure rather than history.",
    "Deinstitutionalisation is not itself a treatment. Where community support was properly funded, outcomes improved substantially. Where it was not, people moved from one poor setting to another."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Define normalisation and name its originator.","The making available to people with intellectual disability of the patterns and conditions of everyday life which are as close as possible to the norms of mainstream society. Formulated by Bengt Nirje in Sweden in 1969."],
    ["What is Social Role Valorisation?","Wolfensberger's 1983 reformulation of normalisation, holding that the goal is to enable devalued people to occupy socially valued roles, because valued social roles bring with them the good things of life, and because devaluation rather than impairment drives poor treatment."],
    ["Name O'Brien's five service accomplishments.","Community presence, choice, competence, respect, and community participation."],
    ["What was Winterbourne View and what did it produce?","A private assessment and treatment unit where systematic abuse of people with learning disability was exposed by BBC Panorama in 2011. It produced the Transforming Care programme, aimed at closing such units in favour of community support, which has repeatedly missed its targets."],
    ["Distinguish impairment from disability in the social model.","Impairment is the functional limitation in the individual. Disability is the disadvantage created by the way society is organised. On this account a person is disabled by inaccessible arrangements rather than by their impairment."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Ely Hospital inquiry, 1969, was the first major UK scandal and led to the Hospital Advisory Service.",
    "Better Services for the Mentally Handicapped, 1971, first set hospital reduction targets.",
    "An Ordinary Life, King's Fund, 1980, set out ordinary housing with support brought to the person.",
    "Valuing People, 2001: rights, independence, choice and inclusion.",
    "Person-centred planning starts from the person's own goals rather than service categories.",
    "Deinstitutionalisation improves outcome only where community support is adequately funded."
  ],
  refs:["oxid","semid","companion"] };

TEACH["13.1.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"There is a long argument about whether people with intellectual disability should have their own psychiatric services or use the ordinary ones. The honest answer is that mainstream services usually cannot adjust enough, and specialist services are usually too small to cover everything.",
  teach:[
    "The case for specialist services: expertise in atypical presentation, in communication, in behavioural phenotypes, in epilepsy and in polypharmacy; and the demonstrable failure of mainstream services to make reasonable adjustments.",
    "The case for mainstream services: normalisation implies using the same services as everyone else, specialist services can become segregated and hard to leave, and separate provision risks lower standards through invisibility.",
    "The practical settlement in England is a specialist community learning disability team supporting access to mainstream services, rather than replacing them.",
    "The multidisciplinary community team typically includes psychiatry, clinical psychology, learning disability nursing, speech and language therapy, occupational therapy, physiotherapy, dietetics and social work.",
    "Speech and language therapy and positive behaviour support are central rather than peripheral in this specialty, unlike in general adult psychiatry.",
    "Inpatient provision: assessment and treatment units, and forensic learning disability services at low, medium and high security. Policy since Transforming Care has been to reduce the former substantially.",
    "The Equality Act 2010 imposes a duty to make reasonable adjustments. In practice this means longer appointments, accessible information, quiet waiting areas, flexible appointment times, and involvement of carers.",
    "Health inequality in this population is severe and documented. People with intellectual disability die substantially younger than the general population, and a large share of those deaths are judged avoidable.",
    "Death by Indifference, Mencap, 2007, described six deaths attributed to institutional discrimination in the NHS and prompted an independent inquiry.",
    "The Confidential Inquiry into premature deaths of people with learning disabilities, 2013, and the subsequent LeDeR programme quantified the mortality gap and identified recurring failures in diagnosis, in reasonable adjustment and in coordination.",
    "Annual health checks for people aged fourteen and over on the learning disability register are the principal preventive intervention in UK primary care, and uptake remains incomplete.",
    "The Oliver McGowan Mandatory Training on learning disability and autism became a statutory requirement for health and social care staff under the Health and Care Act 2022, following the death of Oliver McGowan.",
    "STOMP, Stopping Over Medication of People with a learning disability, autism or both, is the national programme addressing the very high rates of psychotropic prescribing without a corresponding psychiatric diagnosis.",
    "Transition from child to adult services is a recognised failure point, with services organised on different criteria and thresholds either side of the boundary."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What does the Equality Act 2010 require of health services for people with intellectual disability?","Reasonable adjustments: in practice longer appointments, accessible information in easy-read or other formats, quiet waiting environments, flexible appointment times and appropriate involvement of carers."],
    ["What is the LeDeR programme?","The national Learning Disabilities Mortality Review programme, following the 2013 Confidential Inquiry, which reviews deaths of people with learning disability to quantify the mortality gap and identify recurring failures in diagnosis, reasonable adjustment and coordination of care."],
    ["What is STOMP?","Stopping Over Medication of People with a learning disability, autism or both: the national programme addressing high rates of psychotropic prescribing, particularly antipsychotics, given without a corresponding psychiatric diagnosis and frequently for behaviour alone."],
    ["What is the Oliver McGowan Mandatory Training?","Statutory training on learning disability and autism required of health and social care staff under the Health and Care Act 2022, introduced following the death of Oliver McGowan."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Death by Indifference, Mencap, 2007, described deaths attributed to institutional discrimination.",
    "People with intellectual disability die substantially younger, with many deaths judged avoidable.",
    "Annual health checks apply from age fourteen for those on the learning disability register.",
    "Speech and language therapy and positive behaviour support are central to the specialist team.",
    "Transition from child to adult services is a recognised systemic failure point."
  ],
  refs:["oxid","semid","nice"] };

TEACH["13.2.1"] = { free:true,
  simple:"Roughly one person in fifty has an intellectual disability, but only a fraction of those are known to services. And among those who are, mental illness is two to three times commoner than in everyone else, for reasons that are mostly not mysterious.",
  teach:[
    "Definition requires three elements: significant impairment of intellectual functioning, significant impairment of adaptive or social functioning, and onset during the developmental period.",
    "All three are required. A person with a low IQ who functions well socially does not meet the definition, and an acquired impairment in adult life is not intellectual disability.",
    "IQ bands, approximate: mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34, profound below 20. IQ alone never establishes the diagnosis, and ICD-11 explicitly de-emphasises it in favour of functioning.",
    "Prevalence: administrative prevalence, those known to services, runs at around 0.5 per cent in the UK. True prevalence including unidentified mild disability is estimated at around 2 per cent.",
    "Mild intellectual disability accounts for the great majority, around 85 per cent of all cases, and is disproportionately associated with social deprivation.",
    "Severe and profound intellectual disability is more evenly distributed across social classes, because it is more often caused by identifiable biological pathology rather than by the interaction of polygenic and environmental factors.",
    "Life expectancy is reduced and falls further with increasing severity, though it has risen substantially over recent decades, particularly in Down syndrome.",
    "Psychiatric comorbidity: rates of mental disorder are approximately two to three times the general population rate, with estimates varying widely by case definition and by whether challenging behaviour is counted.",
    "Epilepsy affects a large minority, and prevalence rises with severity, reaching around half in profound disability against around one per cent in the general population.",
    "Autism co-occurs in a substantial proportion, rising with severity of disability, and ADHD is also markedly over-represented.",
    "Schizophrenia is around three times more common than in the general population; bipolar disorder and depression are also more common.",
    "Why the rates are raised: shared biological aetiology, epilepsy and its treatment, sensory impairment, communication difficulty, low self-esteem and repeated failure, restricted social networks, poverty, and very high rates of abuse and bullying.",
    "Life events matter and are under-recognised. Bereavement in particular is frequently concealed from people with intellectual disability, who are then excluded from funerals and rituals and present later with behavioural change.",
    "Abuse is a major aetiological factor. People with intellectual disability experience physical, sexual and financial abuse at far higher rates than the general population and are far less likely to be believed or to secure a prosecution.",
    "Diagnostic overshadowing suppresses recorded rates: symptoms are attributed to the disability rather than recognised as a treatable illness, so real prevalence is probably higher than measured prevalence."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["State the three diagnostic requirements for intellectual disability.","Significant impairment of intellectual functioning; significant impairment of adaptive or social functioning; and onset during the developmental period. All three are required, and IQ alone never establishes the diagnosis."],
    ["Give the administrative and true prevalence of intellectual disability, and the distribution by severity.","Administrative prevalence, those known to services, is around 0.5 per cent in the UK; true prevalence including unidentified mild disability is estimated at around 2 per cent. Mild disability accounts for around 85 per cent of cases."],
    ["Why is psychiatric disorder more common in intellectual disability?","Shared biological aetiology, epilepsy and antiepileptic treatment, sensory impairment, communication difficulty, repeated experience of failure, restricted social networks, poverty, and very high rates of abuse and bullying. Recorded rates are further affected by diagnostic overshadowing."],
    ["What is the relationship between severity of disability and epilepsy prevalence?","Epilepsy prevalence rises with severity, from around one per cent in the general population to approximately half of people with profound intellectual disability."],
    ["How does the social class distribution differ between mild and severe intellectual disability?","Mild intellectual disability is disproportionately associated with social deprivation. Severe and profound disability is distributed more evenly across social classes because it more often arises from identifiable biological pathology."]
  ],
  note:[
    "IQ bands: mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34, profound below 20.",
    "ICD-11 de-emphasises IQ in favour of functional assessment.",
    "Schizophrenia is around three times more common than in the general population.",
    "Bereavement is frequently concealed from people with intellectual disability, who then present with behavioural change.",
    "Abuse rates are far higher and prosecution rates far lower than in the general population."
  ],
  refs:["oxid","semid","icd11"] };

TEACH["13.2.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Causes divide into what happened before birth, during birth, and after. In severe disability a cause is usually found; in mild disability it usually is not, and the honest answer is that most mild disability is the ordinary bottom end of a normal distribution plus disadvantage.",
  teach:[
    "Classification by timing: prenatal, perinatal and postnatal. Prenatal causes predominate, particularly in severe disability.",
    "A specific cause is identified in the great majority of severe cases and in a minority of mild cases. Chromosomal microarray is now the first-line genetic investigation, superseding karyotype.",
    "Down syndrome, trisomy 21, is the commonest chromosomal cause. Incidence rises steeply with maternal age. Three mechanisms: non-disjunction in around 95 per cent, translocation in around 4 per cent, and mosaicism in around 1 per cent.",
    "Only translocation Down syndrome carries a high recurrence risk requiring parental karyotyping, which is why the mechanism matters clinically.",
    "Down syndrome features: hypotonia, single palmar crease, epicanthic folds, upslanting palpebral fissures, Brushfield spots, congenital heart disease in around 40 per cent, duodenal atresia, hypothyroidism, atlantoaxial instability, and early-onset Alzheimer's disease.",
    "Almost all people with Down syndrome develop Alzheimer neuropathology by age forty, because the amyloid precursor protein gene sits on chromosome 21 and is present in triple dose. Clinical dementia typically appears in the fifties.",
    "Fragile X syndrome is the commonest inherited cause of intellectual disability. A CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in FMR1 on the X chromosome silences the gene.",
    "Repeat sizes: normal under 45, premutation 55 to 200, full mutation over 200 with methylation and silencing. Anticipation occurs through maternal transmission because the premutation expands in the female germline.",
    "Fragile X features: long face, large everted ears, macro-orchidism after puberty, joint laxity, mitral valve prolapse, gaze aversion, social anxiety, hand-flapping and a high rate of autism and ADHD.",
    "Premutation carriers are not unaffected. They may develop fragile X-associated tremor ataxia syndrome, FXTAS, in later life, and fragile X-associated primary ovarian insufficiency in women.",
    "Fetal alcohol spectrum disorder is the commonest preventable cause of intellectual disability. Features: microcephaly, short palpebral fissures, smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, growth restriction, and a characteristic profile of executive dysfunction and social difficulty.",
    "No safe threshold of alcohol in pregnancy has been established, and UK guidance advises abstinence throughout.",
    "Other prenatal causes: congenital infection, classically toxoplasmosis, rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpes and syphilis; maternal iodine deficiency; teratogens including sodium valproate; and untreated maternal phenylketonuria.",
    "Sodium valproate in pregnancy carries a large risk of both major congenital malformation and neurodevelopmental impairment, and it is now subject to the pregnancy prevention programme.",
    "Perinatal causes: hypoxic ischaemic encephalopathy, extreme prematurity, kernicterus, and neonatal hypoglycaemia. Postnatal causes: meningitis and encephalitis, traumatic brain injury including non-accidental injury, lead poisoning, and prolonged severe neglect.",
    "Metabolic and single-gene conditions: phenylketonuria, detected by newborn screening and preventable by diet; Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes from chromosome 15 imprinting; Williams syndrome from 7q11.23 deletion; Rett syndrome from MECP2; and tuberous sclerosis.",
    "The concept of secondary handicap, from Valerie Sinason, describes the additional disability that arises from the psychological response to the primary impairment and to how others react to it: exaggerated helplessness, withdrawal, or the adoption of a stupid persona as a defence.",
    "Sinason's clinical point is that some of what looks like the disability itself is a defensive adaptation to trauma and devaluation, and is therefore modifiable by psychological work."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Give the three cytogenetic mechanisms of Down syndrome and their clinical significance.","Non-disjunction in around 95 per cent, translocation in around 4 per cent, and mosaicism in around 1 per cent. Only translocation carries a high recurrence risk, requiring parental karyotyping, which is why establishing the mechanism matters."],
    ["Explain the genetics of fragile X syndrome.","A CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome. Normal is under 45 repeats, premutation 55 to 200, and full mutation over 200 repeats with methylation and gene silencing. Anticipation occurs through maternal transmission because the premutation expands in the female germline."],
    ["Why does Down syndrome carry a very high risk of Alzheimer's disease?","The amyloid precursor protein gene is on chromosome 21 and is therefore present in triple dose. Almost all people with Down syndrome have Alzheimer neuropathology by age forty, with clinical dementia typically emerging in the fifties."],
    ["Describe fetal alcohol spectrum disorder.","The commonest preventable cause of intellectual disability. Features include microcephaly, short palpebral fissures, a smooth philtrum, a thin upper lip and growth restriction, with a characteristic profile of executive dysfunction and social difficulty. No safe threshold of alcohol in pregnancy has been established."],
    ["What is secondary handicap?","Sinason's concept of the additional disability arising from the psychological response to the primary impairment and to others' reactions to it, including exaggerated helplessness, withdrawal, or the adoption of a stupid persona as a defence. It is modifiable by psychological work."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Chromosomal microarray is the first-line genetic investigation, superseding karyotype.",
    "Fragile X premutation carriers may develop FXTAS and primary ovarian insufficiency.",
    "Sodium valproate in pregnancy carries high risks of malformation and neurodevelopmental impairment.",
    "Phenylketonuria is detected by newborn screening and prevented by dietary treatment.",
    "Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes arise from imprinting at chromosome 15.",
    "Atlantoaxial instability and hypothyroidism are specific Down syndrome associations."
  ],
  refs:["semgen","oxid","semid"] };

TEACH["13.3.1"] = { free:true,
  simple:"If your only tool is asking questions, you will get almost nothing. The assessment has to be rebuilt around what this person can actually do: shorter sentences, longer silences, concrete words, pictures, and a great deal of information from the people who know them.",
  teach:[
    "The first task is to establish the person's communication level, not to take a history. Ask the carer how the person communicates, what they understand, and what they use to make themselves understood.",
    "Practical adjustments: allow much longer, meet in a familiar place, reduce distraction, and see the person alone as well as with the carer, since abuse and unhappiness are rarely disclosed in front of the person being complained about.",
    "Language: short sentences, one idea at a time, concrete rather than abstract, active rather than passive voice, no metaphor, and no double negatives.",
    "Allow long pauses. Processing time is longer, and filling the silence with a second question guarantees you get an answer to neither.",
    "Avoid leading questions and yes or no questions, both of which produce acquiescence rather than information. Acquiescence, the tendency to agree, is a well-documented response bias in this population.",
    "Nay-saying and recency effects are also documented: some people consistently disagree, and many people choose the last option offered. Vary the order of options to test this.",
    "Use open questions where possible, then multiple choice with the options varied, then closed questions only as a last resort. Cross-check important answers by asking differently later.",
    "Non-verbal tools: photographs, symbols, Talking Mats, drawings, body maps for pain and for abuse, and easy-read materials.",
    "Communication systems in use include Makaton, a signing and symbol system, and PECS, the Picture Exchange Communication System. Objects of reference are used at profound levels.",
    "Intensive interaction is an approach for people at the earliest communication levels, using imitation and responsiveness to build the foundations of interaction rather than to transmit content.",
    "Collateral history is not supplementary here, it is central. Establish what has changed, when it changed, and what else changed around the same time.",
    "Ask the carer for a description of behaviour rather than an interpretation. He is aggressive is an interpretation; he hit out when the television was turned off is a description you can work with.",
    "Always assess physical health, pain, constipation, dental disease, otitis, reflux, and sensory impairment as part of a psychiatric assessment. Behavioural change is frequently a report of pain in someone who cannot report pain.",
    "Consider medication, including recent changes and antiepileptics, and consider seizures, including non-convulsive status, in any acute change in presentation.",
    "Capacity is assessed as in any other person, decision by decision, with all practicable support given first. Intellectual disability never establishes incapacity.",
    "Supported decision-making means providing the information in a usable form and giving the time and support to weigh it, before concluding anything about capacity."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is acquiescence and how do you counter it?","The well-documented tendency of people with intellectual disability to agree with the interviewer regardless of the content of the question. Counter it by avoiding leading and yes-or-no questions, using open questions, offering multiple choice with the order of options varied, and cross-checking important answers by asking differently later."],
    ["Name four practical communication adjustments in assessment.","Allow substantially longer for the appointment; use short concrete sentences with one idea at a time; allow long pauses for processing without filling the silence; and use non-verbal aids such as photographs, symbols, Talking Mats or body maps."],
    ["Why must the person be seen alone as well as with the carer?","Because abuse, unhappiness and complaints about care are rarely disclosed in the presence of the person being complained about, who is frequently the carer accompanying them."],
    ["What must always be excluded before attributing behavioural change to psychiatric illness?","Physical causes: pain of any origin, constipation, dental disease, otitis, reflux, sensory impairment, medication change, and seizures including non-convulsive status. Behavioural change is often the only available report of pain."],
    ["How should a carer be asked about behaviour?","For a description rather than an interpretation. He hit out when the television was turned off is usable data; he is aggressive is not."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Establish the communication level before taking any history.",
    "Nay-saying and recency effects also affect responses; vary the order of options offered.",
    "Makaton is a signing and symbol system; PECS is the Picture Exchange Communication System.",
    "Intensive interaction builds the foundations of interaction at the earliest communication levels.",
    "Capacity is decision-specific; intellectual disability never establishes incapacity."
  ],
  refs:["oxid","semid","nice"] };

TEACH["13.3.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"The single most important idea in this specialty has an ugly name and a simple meaning. Diagnostic overshadowing is what happens when a doctor sees the disability and stops looking, so a treatable illness is written off as just how he is.",
  teach:[
    "Diagnostic overshadowing is the attribution of symptoms to the intellectual disability itself rather than to a coexisting and treatable disorder. It is the commonest diagnostic error in this population and it applies to physical illness as much as to psychiatric illness.",
    "Presentation changes with the level of disability. In mild disability, presentation resembles the general adult picture. In severe and profound disability, psychiatric illness presents almost entirely through observed behaviour.",
    "The core skill is establishing a baseline. Illness is a change from this person's usual state, and without knowing the usual state you cannot recognise the change.",
    "Depression in intellectual disability presents as social withdrawal, loss of skills, irritability and aggression, increased self-injury, tearfulness, changes in sleep and appetite, and psychomotor change. Reported low mood may be entirely absent.",
    "Loss of previously acquired skills is a red flag that should never be dismissed. It indicates depression, dementia, hypothyroidism, epilepsy or abuse until proven otherwise.",
    "Mania presents as overactivity, reduced sleep, irritability more than elation, increased vocalisation, disinhibition and aggression.",
    "Psychosis is difficult to assess. Hallucinations and delusions require a level of language to report, and below that level psychosis is inferred from behaviour: apparent responding to unseen stimuli, unexplained fear, and new withdrawal.",
    "The differential must include the person's ordinary self-talk and imaginary companions, which are more common and more persistent in this population and are not psychotic phenomena.",
    "Anxiety presents as avoidance, agitation, somatic complaints, increased ritual and increased challenging behaviour. Behavioural equivalents replace verbal report throughout.",
    "Catatonia is under-recognised, over-represented in autism and intellectual disability, and treatable. Look for new immobility, mutism, posturing, negativism, waxy flexibility, and a marked slowing from baseline.",
    "Autistic catatonic deterioration is a recognised presentation in adolescence and young adulthood in autism, with progressive slowing and loss of function, and it responds to benzodiazepines and to ECT.",
    "Challenging behaviour is not a diagnosis. It is a description of behaviour that, by its intensity, frequency or duration, threatens the safety of the person or others, or limits access to ordinary community facilities. Emerson's definition.",
    "Challenging behaviour has a cause, and the cause may be physical illness, pain, psychiatric illness, communication of an unmet need, environmental, or a learned response. It should never be treated as the endpoint of an assessment.",
    "Diagnostic criteria adapted for this population: DC-LD, the Diagnostic Criteria for Psychiatric Disorders for Use with Adults with Learning Disabilities, from the Royal College of Psychiatrists, and the DM-ID adaptation of DSM criteria.",
    "Screening instruments: PAS-ADD, the Psychiatric Assessment Schedule for Adults with Developmental Disabilities, with its Mini PAS-ADD and PAS-ADD Checklist versions; and the Aberrant Behavior Checklist for behaviour.",
    "Dementia in Down syndrome requires baseline assessment before decline, since standard cognitive tests are unusable. The DLD, Dementia Questionnaire for Learning Disabilities, and the DSQIID are the informant-based instruments used."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Define diagnostic overshadowing.","The attribution of symptoms to a person's intellectual disability rather than to a coexisting and treatable disorder. It is the commonest diagnostic error in this population and applies to physical as well as psychiatric illness."],
    ["How does depression present in severe intellectual disability?","Through observed behaviour rather than reported mood: social withdrawal, loss of previously acquired skills, irritability and aggression, increased self-injury, tearfulness, and changes in sleep, appetite and psychomotor activity. Verbal report of low mood may be entirely absent."],
    ["Define challenging behaviour.","Emerson's definition: behaviour of such intensity, frequency or duration that the physical safety of the person or others is placed in serious jeopardy, or behaviour likely to seriously limit or deny access to ordinary community facilities. It is a description, not a diagnosis, and always has a cause."],
    ["Which diagnostic systems are adapted for this population?","DC-LD, the Diagnostic Criteria for Psychiatric Disorders for Use with Adults with Learning Disabilities, published by the Royal College of Psychiatrists; and DM-ID, the diagnostic manual adapting DSM criteria for intellectual disability."],
    ["Why is catatonia important in this population?","It is over-represented in autism and intellectual disability, frequently unrecognised, and highly treatable. Look for new immobility, mutism, posturing, negativism, waxy flexibility and marked slowing from baseline. Autistic catatonic deterioration in adolescence responds to benzodiazepines and to ECT."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Loss of previously acquired skills indicates depression, dementia, hypothyroidism, epilepsy or abuse until proven otherwise.",
    "Mania presents with irritability more often than elation.",
    "Self-talk and imaginary companions are commoner in this population and are not psychotic phenomena.",
    "PAS-ADD is the principal psychiatric screening instrument, with Mini and Checklist versions.",
    "Dementia assessment in Down syndrome requires a baseline recorded before any decline."
  ],
  refs:["semid","oxid","icd11"] };

TEACH["13.3.3"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Instead of asking what is wrong with this behaviour, behavioural psychology asks what the behaviour is for. Almost every persistent behaviour is achieving something, and once you know what, you can teach a better way to achieve the same thing.",
  teach:[
    "Functional analysis rests on the ABC framework: Antecedents, what happened before; Behaviour, described precisely; and Consequences, what followed and what the person obtained or avoided.",
    "The four established functions of behaviour: escape or avoidance of demand, attention, tangible gain, and sensory or automatic reinforcement.",
    "Function is not obvious from appearance. Two people can hit out for entirely different reasons, and the intervention differs completely. Topography does not determine treatment; function does.",
    "Establishing operations, or setting events, are conditions that change the value of a reinforcer: pain, hunger, poor sleep, illness, a change of staff. They explain why the same trigger produces the behaviour on one day and not another.",
    "Descriptive assessment uses direct observation and ABC charts. Experimental functional analysis manipulates conditions systematically and is the most rigorous method, but is rarely practicable outside specialist settings.",
    "Common errors in ABC recording: recording only the incident, recording interpretation rather than description, and recording only when the behaviour occurs so that the conditions in which it does not occur are never captured.",
    "Self-injurious behaviour is often maintained by automatic reinforcement and may have a biological substrate. It is strongly associated with particular syndromes and with sensory impairment.",
    "The gate control theory and endogenous opioid hypotheses have been proposed for self-injury, and naltrexone has been trialled on that basis with inconsistent results.",
    "Positive Behaviour Support is the current framework and it is explicitly value-led as well as technical. It requires a functional assessment, environmental and antecedent change, teaching of functionally equivalent alternative skills, and a reduction in restrictive practice.",
    "PBS emphasises primary prevention: changing the environment so the behaviour becomes unnecessary, rather than managing it after it starts.",
    "Functional communication training teaches a communicative response that achieves the same outcome as the behaviour, more easily. If hitting out gets a break, teach a break card that works faster and more reliably.",
    "Differential reinforcement schedules: DRO reinforces the absence of the behaviour, DRA reinforces an alternative behaviour, and DRI reinforces a behaviour incompatible with it.",
    "Aversive procedures, punishment and response cost are not part of contemporary practice. Restrictive interventions must be a last resort, proportionate, time-limited and recorded, under the Restraint Reduction Network standards.",
    "Restrictive practice includes physical restraint, mechanical restraint, seclusion, long-term segregation and rapid tranquillisation. Prone restraint is specifically discouraged in national guidance.",
    "Active support is the staff practice model that increases engagement in meaningful activity, which is itself among the more effective behavioural interventions because unoccupied time is a major setting event.",
    "The plan must be written so that support staff can implement it. A behaviour plan that only a psychologist can follow will not be followed."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Name the four functions of behaviour.","Escape or avoidance of demand; attention; tangible gain; and sensory or automatic reinforcement. Function, rather than the appearance of the behaviour, determines the intervention."],
    ["What are establishing operations, and why do they matter?","Setting events such as pain, hunger, poor sleep, illness or a change of staff, which alter the value of a reinforcer. They explain why an identical trigger produces the behaviour on one day and not another."],
    ["Describe Positive Behaviour Support.","A values-led framework requiring functional assessment, environmental and antecedent change, teaching of functionally equivalent alternative skills, and reduction of restrictive practice, with emphasis on preventing the behaviour becoming necessary rather than managing it once started."],
    ["What is functional communication training?","Teaching a communicative response that achieves the same outcome as the challenging behaviour, more easily and reliably. If hitting out obtains a break from demand, the person is taught a break request that works faster."],
    ["Distinguish DRO, DRA and DRI.","Differential reinforcement of other behaviour reinforces the absence of the target behaviour; differential reinforcement of alternative behaviour reinforces a specified alternative; differential reinforcement of incompatible behaviour reinforces a behaviour that cannot occur simultaneously with the target."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Experimental functional analysis is the most rigorous method but is rarely practicable outside specialist settings.",
    "ABC recording must capture conditions in which the behaviour does not occur, not only incidents.",
    "Restrictive interventions must be a last resort, proportionate, time-limited and recorded.",
    "Prone restraint is specifically discouraged in national guidance.",
    "Active support increases engagement in meaningful activity and reduces unoccupied time as a setting event.",
    "A behaviour plan must be written so that support staff can actually implement it."
  ],
  refs:["semid","oxid","nice"] };

TEACH["13.3.4"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Everything psychiatry has works here, but everything needs adapting. The bigger problem is the opposite one: for decades, the main treatment offered has been an antipsychotic given to someone with no psychiatric diagnosis at all, simply because their behaviour was difficult.",
  teach:[
    "The over-medication problem is the central prescribing issue in this specialty. A large proportion of people with intellectual disability receive psychotropic medication, and a substantial share of those have no corresponding psychiatric diagnosis.",
    "STOMP, launched in 2016, is the national campaign to stop over-medication of people with a learning disability, autism or both. STAMP is its counterpart for children and young people.",
    "NICE guidance on challenging behaviour, NG11, is explicit: antipsychotics should be considered only if psychological or other interventions do not produce change within an agreed timeframe, or if the risk is severe, and only alongside those interventions.",
    "Where an antipsychotic is used for behaviour, NICE requires a single drug at the lowest effective dose, a documented target and timeframe, review at four weeks, and a plan to reduce and stop if there is no clear benefit by twelve weeks.",
    "The evidence for antipsychotics in challenging behaviour without psychosis is weak. The Tyrer NACHBID trial in 2008 found no advantage for risperidone or haloperidol over placebo, and placebo performed numerically better at several time points.",
    "Risperidone has the best evidence in irritability and aggression associated with autism, and is licensed in that narrow indication, but the effect is on irritability rather than on core features.",
    "Adverse effects are more likely and less likely to be reported: people may not be able to describe akathisia, constipation, sedation or sexual dysfunction, and they are then attributed to the disability. Monitoring must be systematic rather than symptom-triggered.",
    "Antiepileptic interactions and the psychiatric effects of antiepileptics matter greatly, given the prevalence of epilepsy. Levetiracetam is associated with irritability and behavioural change; topiramate with cognitive slowing.",
    "Lowered seizure threshold with antipsychotics and antidepressants is a real consideration; clozapine and chlorpromazine carry the highest risk, and among antidepressants bupropion is the notable one.",
    "Psychological treatment is effective and under-provided. Adapted CBT works in mild and moderate disability for anxiety and depression, with simplified language, more concrete material, visual aids, shorter sessions and carer involvement.",
    "Anger management adapted for intellectual disability has a reasonable evidence base, and adapted CBT for anger has been the most studied psychological intervention in this population.",
    "Psychodynamic and arts psychotherapies are used, particularly around loss, abuse and disability itself, and Sinason's work on secondary handicap sits here.",
    "Behavioural interventions and Positive Behaviour Support are first-line for challenging behaviour, not adjunctive.",
    "Systemic and family work matters because the person's environment is frequently the paid care system, and the system's responses maintain much behaviour.",
    "The multidisciplinary approach is not decorative: speech and language therapy makes psychological work possible, occupational therapy addresses sensory needs and daily structure, and physical health input addresses the causes that present as behaviour.",
    "Reasonable adjustments to treatment are a legal duty: longer appointments, accessible information, and consistent staff wherever possible."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What does NICE NG11 say about antipsychotics for challenging behaviour?","They should be considered only if psychological or other interventions do not produce change within an agreed timeframe, or if the risk is severe, and only alongside those interventions. A single drug at the lowest effective dose, with a documented target and timeframe, review at four weeks, and a plan to reduce and stop if there is no clear benefit by twelve weeks."],
    ["What did the NACHBID trial find?","Tyrer and colleagues, 2008, found no advantage for risperidone or haloperidol over placebo in aggressive challenging behaviour in intellectual disability, with placebo performing numerically better at several time points."],
    ["What are STOMP and STAMP?","STOMP is the national campaign, launched in 2016, to stop over-medication of people with a learning disability, autism or both. STAMP is its counterpart for children and young people."],
    ["How is CBT adapted for intellectual disability?","Simplified concrete language, more visual and pictorial material, shorter and more frequent sessions, more repetition, greater use of behavioural over cognitive components, and involvement of a carer to support between-session work. Effective in mild and moderate disability for anxiety, depression and anger."],
    ["Why is adverse effect monitoring especially important in this population?","Because people may be unable to report akathisia, constipation, sedation, pain or sexual dysfunction, and such effects are then misattributed to the disability itself. Monitoring must therefore be systematic and scheduled rather than triggered by complaint."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Risperidone is licensed for irritability and aggression in autism and affects irritability, not core features.",
    "Levetiracetam is associated with irritability and behavioural change; topiramate with cognitive slowing.",
    "Clozapine and chlorpromazine carry the highest seizure risk among antipsychotics.",
    "Positive Behaviour Support is first-line for challenging behaviour, not adjunctive.",
    "The care system's own responses frequently maintain the behaviour being treated."
  ],
  refs:["nice","maudsley","semid","oxid"] };

TEACH["13.3.5"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Some genetic conditions come with a predictable pattern of behaviour, as reliably as they come with a facial appearance. Knowing the pattern tells you what to expect, what to screen for, and what not to blame on the person.",
  teach:[
    "A behavioural phenotype is the characteristic pattern of behavioural, cognitive and psychiatric features associated with a specific genetic disorder, occurring at a rate significantly above chance.",
    "The phenotype is probabilistic, not deterministic. It raises the likelihood of a feature; it does not guarantee it, and it does not license disregarding the individual.",
    "Prader-Willi syndrome, loss of paternal chromosome 15q11-13: neonatal hypotonia and poor feeding, then insatiable hyperphagia from early childhood, obesity, short stature, hypogonadism, skin picking, temper outbursts, and marked obsessional features.",
    "Prader-Willi carries a high rate of psychotic illness in adulthood, particularly in the maternal uniparental disomy subtype, where rates approach half.",
    "Angelman syndrome, loss of maternal chromosome 15q11-13: severe disability, absent or minimal speech, ataxic jerky gait, a happy sociable demeanour with paroxysmal laughter, epilepsy, and a characteristic EEG.",
    "Prader-Willi and Angelman arise from the same chromosomal region and differ by parent of origin. They are the classical teaching example of genomic imprinting.",
    "Fragile X: social anxiety, gaze aversion, hand-flapping and hand-biting, perseverative speech, hyperarousal, a high rate of autism and very high rates of ADHD. Social avoidance with a wish for social contact distinguishes it from autism.",
    "Down syndrome: relative strength in social functioning, weakness in expressive language and verbal short-term memory, low rates of ADHD and conduct problems, and very high rates of early-onset Alzheimer's disease.",
    "Williams syndrome, 7q11.23 deletion: over-friendliness with strangers, a distinctive expressive social manner, strong verbal and musical ability relative to severe visuospatial impairment, hyperacusis, high anxiety and specific phobia, and supravalvular aortic stenosis.",
    "Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, HPRT deficiency, X-linked: hyperuricaemia, dystonia and choreoathetosis, and compulsive severe self-mutilation, characteristically biting of the lips and fingers, which the person often does not wish to do and asks to be restrained from.",
    "Smith-Magenis syndrome, 17p11.2 deletion: self-hugging, onychotillomania and polyembolokoilamania, severely disrupted sleep with an inverted melatonin rhythm, and marked self-injury.",
    "Cri du chat syndrome, 5p deletion: the characteristic cat-like cry in infancy, severe disability, hyperactivity and self-injury.",
    "Velocardiofacial syndrome, 22q11.2 deletion: cleft palate, cardiac anomalies, mild disability, and a very high rate of schizophrenia, of the order of a quarter to a third, making it one of the strongest known genetic risk factors for psychosis.",
    "Rett syndrome, MECP2, almost exclusively female: normal early development then regression, loss of purposeful hand use with characteristic hand-wringing stereotypies, deceleration of head growth, and severe disability.",
    "Tuberous sclerosis: skin lesions, epilepsy often with infantile spasms, high rates of autism and of ADHD, and behavioural difficulty proportional to seizure burden.",
    "The practical use of phenotypes is anticipatory: screen for psychosis in velocardiofacial and Prader-Willi syndrome, screen for dementia in Down syndrome, address sleep in Smith-Magenis, address food security in Prader-Willi, and treat the person rather than the syndrome."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Define a behavioural phenotype.","The characteristic pattern of behavioural, cognitive and psychiatric features associated with a specific genetic disorder, occurring at a rate significantly greater than chance. It is probabilistic rather than deterministic."],
    ["Contrast Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes.","Both arise from chromosome 15q11-13 and differ by parent of origin, the classical example of genomic imprinting. Prader-Willi results from loss of the paternal contribution and features hyperphagia, obesity, skin picking and a high rate of adult psychosis. Angelman results from loss of the maternal contribution and features severe disability, absent speech, ataxic gait, paroxysmal laughter and epilepsy."],
    ["Which syndrome carries the highest genetic risk of schizophrenia?","Velocardiofacial syndrome, the 22q11.2 deletion, in which schizophrenia occurs in approximately a quarter to a third of affected individuals, making it one of the strongest known genetic risk factors for psychosis."],
    ["Describe the behavioural phenotype of Lesch-Nyhan syndrome.","Compulsive severe self-mutilation, characteristically biting of the lips and fingers, which the person frequently does not wish to perform and asks to be restrained from, together with hyperuricaemia, dystonia and choreoathetosis. X-linked, due to HPRT deficiency."],
    ["Describe the behavioural phenotype of Williams syndrome.","Over-friendliness with strangers and a distinctive expressive social manner, relative strength in verbal and musical ability against severe visuospatial impairment, hyperacusis, high anxiety and specific phobia. Due to 7q11.23 deletion, with supravalvular aortic stenosis."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Prader-Willi psychosis rates approach half in the maternal uniparental disomy subtype.",
    "Fragile X shows social avoidance combined with a wish for social contact, distinguishing it from autism.",
    "Smith-Magenis syndrome features an inverted melatonin rhythm and severely disrupted sleep.",
    "Rett syndrome, MECP2, features regression with hand-wringing stereotypies and head growth deceleration.",
    "Behavioural phenotypes are used anticipatorily, to direct screening and preventive management."
  ],
  refs:["semgen","oxid","semid"] };

TEACH["13.3.6"] = { free:false,
  simple:"When a child is born with a disability, the family receives two things at once: a child they love, and the loss of the child they had expected. Both are real, and pretending only the first exists is one of the ways professionals make things worse.",
  teach:[
    "Chronic sorrow, described by Olshansky in 1962, is the enduring and recurrent grief experienced by parents of a child with a disability. It is a normal response, not pathological, and it recurs at developmental milestones the child does not reach.",
    "The recurrence pattern matters clinically. Grief returns when peers start school, pass exams, leave home, marry or have children, and a parent presenting distressed at such a moment is not relapsing but responding.",
    "Stage models of adjustment, borrowed from bereavement, describe shock, denial, anger, bargaining, sadness and acceptance. They are useful as vocabulary but the evidence does not support fixed sequential stages.",
    "The disclosure of diagnosis has lasting effects on the family's relationship with services. Parents remember how they were told for decades, and being told badly predicts later mistrust of professionals.",
    "Good disclosure practice: both parents together where possible, in private, without interruption, early rather than late, with the child present, with honest information, and with a follow-up appointment arranged.",
    "Practical burden: caring is physically demanding, financially costly, and rarely time-limited. Many parents reduce or leave paid employment, with lifelong consequences for income and pension.",
    "Parental mental health is affected. Rates of depression and anxiety in parents, particularly mothers, of children with intellectual disability are consistently raised.",
    "Behaviour, rather than the degree of disability, is the strongest predictor of parental stress. A very disabled child who is settled places less strain than a less disabled child with severe challenging behaviour.",
    "Siblings: outcomes are mixed rather than uniformly negative. Reported effects include increased maturity, empathy and tolerance alongside restricted parental attention, embarrassment, worry, and anticipated future caring responsibility.",
    "The what happens when we die question is the central long-term family anxiety, and it is under-addressed by services. Future planning, financial arrangements and sibling expectations should be raised proactively.",
    "Carers have statutory rights. Under the Care Act 2014 a carer is entitled to a carer's assessment in their own right, irrespective of the cared-for person's eligibility.",
    "Short breaks and respite are the most frequently requested and most frequently cut form of support, and their withdrawal is a common precipitant of placement breakdown.",
    "Positive contributions are real and should be named rather than assumed: many families describe strengthened relationships, changed priorities and a sense of meaning, and hearing only a deficit account from professionals is itself alienating.",
    "Cultural context shapes meaning: explanatory models of causation, expectations about family caring, shame and disclosure outside the family, and willingness to accept external support all vary and must be asked about rather than assumed."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is chronic sorrow?","Olshansky's 1962 description of the enduring and recurrent grief experienced by parents of a child with a disability. It is a normal rather than pathological response and recurs at the developmental milestones the child does not reach."],
    ["What predicts parental stress most strongly?","The child's challenging behaviour rather than the degree of intellectual disability. A severely disabled but settled child places less strain on the family than a less disabled child with severe behavioural difficulty."],
    ["Describe good practice in disclosing a diagnosis of disability.","Both parents together where possible, in private and without interruption, early rather than late, with the child present, with honest and complete information, and with a follow-up appointment arranged. Parents recall the manner of disclosure for decades and poor disclosure predicts later mistrust of services."],
    ["What rights do family carers have under the Care Act 2014?","A carer is entitled to a carer's assessment in their own right, irrespective of whether the person they care for is eligible for services."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Grief recurs at milestones: school entry, examinations, leaving home, marriage, childbearing.",
    "Depression and anxiety rates are consistently raised in parents, particularly mothers.",
    "Sibling outcomes are mixed rather than uniformly negative.",
    "Long-term future planning is the central unaddressed family anxiety.",
    "Withdrawal of short breaks is a common precipitant of placement breakdown.",
    "Cultural explanatory models of disability must be asked about rather than assumed."
  ],
  refs:["oxid","semid","bhugra"] };

TEACH["13.3.7"] = { free:false,
  simple:"An offender with intellectual disability is usually a person who did not understand, was used by someone else, or could not stop themselves in time. That is not an excuse, but it changes the assessment, the interview, the trial and the treatment.",
  teach:[
    "Prevalence in the criminal justice system is contested. Screened rates in prison are far higher than the community rate, but formally diagnosed intellectual disability is uncommon, and much of the excess is borderline intellectual functioning, IQ 70 to 85, which falls outside the diagnosis and outside services.",
    "The borderline group is the practical problem: too able for learning disability services, too impaired to manage mainstream processes, and consequently unsupported everywhere.",
    "Offences over-represented: arson, sexual offences and criminal damage. Acquisitive and organised offending is under-represented.",
    "Contributing factors: suggestibility and compliance, exploitation by others, poor impulse control, failure to anticipate consequences, limited understanding of the law, and lack of alternative means to achieve goals.",
    "Exploitation is a specific and recurring pattern. Mate crime, in which a person is befriended in order to be used financially, sexually or as a proxy for offending, is a recognised safeguarding category.",
    "Suggestibility and acquiescence create real risk of false confession. Gudjonsson's work established the psychological evidence base and contributed to the safeguards in PACE 1984.",
    "PACE 1984 requires an appropriate adult during police interview of a person who is mentally vulnerable. Identification frequently fails, since intellectual disability is often not apparent at custody reception.",
    "Fitness to plead is more often in issue than in other groups. Effective participation requires understanding of the charge, the plea, the evidence and the proceedings, and the Pritchard threshold is criticised as too low for exactly this population.",
    "Registered intermediaries may be appointed to facilitate communication for vulnerable defendants and witnesses at court, and their use is a specific reasonable adjustment.",
    "Diversion at the earliest point is the policy aim, through liaison and diversion services, and reduces the harm of custody for a group that copes very poorly in prison.",
    "In custody this group is vulnerable to bullying, exploitation, disciplinary sanction for behaviour arising from misunderstanding, and exclusion from offending behaviour programmes that require a level of literacy and abstract reasoning.",
    "Adapted programmes exist: SOTSEC-ID for sexual offending in intellectual disability, and adapted anger management, both with a reasonable evidence base.",
    "Assessment of risk uses adapted instruments. The ARMIDILO-S is designed for sexual offending in intellectual disability; standard instruments including HCR-20 are used with caution and with attention to the validity of items in this population.",
    "Secure forensic learning disability services exist at low, medium and high security, and the policy direction since Transforming Care has been to reduce their inpatient use.",
    "The recurring ethical tension is that a person may be detained far longer through a health pathway than any sentence would have required, because the disability does not resolve and the risk is judged not to have changed."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Which offences are over-represented in offenders with intellectual disability?","Arson, sexual offences and criminal damage. Acquisitive and organised offending is under-represented."],
    ["What is mate crime?","A recognised safeguarding category in which a person with intellectual disability is befriended in order to be exploited financially, sexually, or as a proxy for another person's offending."],
    ["Why is the borderline intellectual functioning group a particular problem?","With an IQ of approximately 70 to 85 they fall outside the diagnosis of intellectual disability and therefore outside specialist services, while remaining too impaired to manage mainstream criminal justice processes unsupported."],
    ["What safeguards apply to a vulnerable suspect at the police station and at court?","At the police station, an appropriate adult must be present during interview under PACE 1984. At court, a registered intermediary may be appointed to facilitate communication for a vulnerable defendant or witness."],
    ["Name adapted assessment and treatment approaches for this group.","ARMIDILO-S for sexual offending risk in intellectual disability, SOTSEC-ID as an adapted sexual offending treatment programme, and adapted anger management. Standard instruments such as HCR-20 are used with caution and attention to item validity."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Screened rates in prison far exceed community rates, but formally diagnosed intellectual disability is uncommon.",
    "Suggestibility and acquiescence create real risk of false confession.",
    "Identification at custody reception frequently fails because disability is not apparent.",
    "The Pritchard fitness threshold is criticised as too low for this population.",
    "Health pathway detention may exceed the length of any sentence that would have been imposed."
  ],
  refs:["oxid","forrester","puriforen"] };
